<article class="help-article p-4">
    <header class="help-article-header mb-4">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined help-article-icon">move_to_inbox</span>
        <h1 class="help-article-title">Fetched Properties</h1>
        <p class="help-article-lead">
            Fetched properties describe a derived relationship-like value that is resolved by a fetch
            instead of being stored as a normal relationship.
        </p>
    </header>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Add a fetched property</h2>
    <ol class="help-steps">
        <li><strong>Select an entity</strong> in the Navigator.</li>
        <li><strong>Open the main menu</strong> and choose <em>Add Fetched Property</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Select the property</strong> and configure it in the Inspector.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="help-heading">When to use one</h2>
    <ul class="help-list">
        <li>Use a fetched property when the target collection should be computed by a predicate.</li>
        <li>Use a relationship when the model needs a stored link with an inverse, delete rule, or ownership semantics.</li>
        <li>Keep the predicate narrow enough that the generated service can resolve it efficiently.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="help-callout">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined">info</span>
        <div>
            <strong>Fetched is not stored.</strong>
            A fetched property gives convenient access to related objects without creating a persisted
            foreign-key style relationship in the model.
        </div>
    </div>
</article>
